config IMAGE_MONITOR
	bool "M-mode runtime monitor"
	default y
	help
	  The resident M-mode firmware: trap handling, drivers and services
	  (SBI and, later, the security services).

if IMAGE_MONITOR

config MONITOR_LOAD_ADDR
	hex "Monitor link/load address"
	default LOADER_NEXT_STAGE_ADDR if IMAGE_LOADER
	default PLATFORM_FW_START

config MONITOR_PIE
	bool "Position-independent monitor"
	default y
	help
	  MONITOR_LOAD_ADDR is then only where the monitor is linked for: it
	  relocates itself to wherever the previous stage put it (and tells
	  where that is in the device tree it passes on). Costs the
	  relocation table in the image.

config MONITOR_SIZE
	hex "Memory reserved for the monitor"
	default PLATFORM_FW_SIZE
	help
	  Hidden from S/U-mode with one PMP NAPOT entry: a power of two, and
	  MONITOR_LOAD_ADDR a multiple of it.

config MONITOR_NEXT_STAGE_ADDR
	hex "Next stage entry point"
	help
	  Where the boot hart enters S-mode, with a0 = hart id and a1 = the
	  device tree address the monitor was given. Loading the next stage
	  is up to the platform (the previous stage, or the simulator). When
	  the first word there is zero the monitor stays idle.

config MONITOR_FDT_ADDR
	hex "Address the device tree is moved to (0: in place)"
	default 0x0
	help
	  The monitor completes and fixes up the device tree it was given,
	  which makes it larger: the monitor's memory becomes reserved,
	  machine-level devices and unmanaged harts are disabled. In place,
	  the memory right after the tree (4 KiB) must be free; otherwise
	  name an address the next stage leaves alone until it has read it.

endif
